The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
defaultClient.getConnectionManager().shutdown();
loadbalancer.shutdown();
```
Reason:
The `shutdown()` method is called on `defaultClient` and `loadbalancer` without checking if either of them is null. If either object is not initialized (null), it results in a `NullPointerException` when trying to call methods on them in the `afterClass` method of `SolrTestCaseJ4`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if(defaultClient != null) {
    defaultClient.getConnectionManager().shutdown();
}
if(loadbalancer != null) {
    loadbalancer.shutdown();
}
```
Reason:
The patch adds null checks before calling the `shutdown()` methods on `defaultClient` and `loadbalancer`. This prevents the `NullPointerException` from occurring if either object is null, allowing the `close()` method to complete without throwing an exception.

